window.docContents[49].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.EffSalt/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___EffSalt":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD › EffSalt.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 6 of 44 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nThe effective 32-byte HKDF salt (DEV-LS25)Trusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use the general-length normaliser effectiveHkdfSalt and fit32 (Lemma 1's one-key first-message Auth game, Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth/Game.lean, effectiveHkdfSaltBytes): read them. effectiveHkdfSalt32 is checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.In MessageKeys::derive_keys, HKDF-Extract computes HMAC-SHA256(salt, ikm) with None mapped to 32 zero bytes. Because HMAC zero-pads keys of length at most 64 to the 64-byte block, any salt of at most 32 bytes acts solely through its 32-byte zero-padded form (None, Some([]), and Some(0^32) coincide); PRF assumptions on salts are stated over this effective 32-byte salt.\n\n\n\ndef fit32L32–45 ↗#Pad with zeros (or truncate) a list to 32 entries.\n\ndef effectiveHkdfSaltL36–38 ↗#Effective 32-byte HKDF salt of an optional salt (none ↦ 0^32, some s ↦ fit32 s).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 40–42): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.effectiveHkdfSalt32\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD/EffSalt.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.EffSalt/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___EffSalt"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.ChainDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___ChainDefs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet › ChainDefs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 13 of 69 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nlibsignal's symmetric ratchet: the chain step and HMAC as a PRF on chain keysTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Models libsignal v0.105.4 ChainKey (rust/protocol/src/ratchet/keys.rs:140–183): one-byte domain-separated HMAC-SHA256 (chainHmac, messageKeySeed = 0x01, chainKeySeed = 0x02) and its PRF view hmacPRF.\n\n\n\nabbrev ChainKeyL36–37 ↗#256-bit chain key as its 32 LE bytes (ChainKey.key : [u8; 32]).\n\ndef chainHmacL39–42 ↗#ChainKey::calculate_base_material: HMAC-SHA256(ck, [b]).\n\ndef messageKeySeedL44–45 ↗#ChainKey::MESSAGE_KEY_SEED (keys.rs:148).\n\ndef chainKeySeedL47–48 ↗#ChainKey::CHAIN_KEY_SEED (keys.rs:149).\n\ndef hmacPRFL50–52 ↗#HMAC-SHA256 on one-byte inputs as a 256-bit PRF (32 LE bytes).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet/ChainDefs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.ChainDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___ChainDefs"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications › LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 4 headline theorems depend on it 84 of 659 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (18)def errTagdef utf8abbrev ServiceIdParserabbrev UuidParserdef hexNibbledef decodeHexdef decodeHex32def parseSimpledef hyphendef parseHyphenateddef urnPrefixdef uuidTryParsedef pniPrefixdef hyphenatedLengthdef tryParseHyphenateddef serviceIdParsedef kindBytedef addrBytes\n\nMemory layouts of libsignal's session state, messages and results in the compiled imageTrusted in part. The theorems of the default library (Computational/Theorems/ outside CliElfLib) use only ServiceIdParser, UuidParser, addrBytes, hyphenatedLength, kindByte, pniPrefix, serviceIdParse, tryParseHyphenated, utf8, uuidTryParse, hexNibble, decodeHex, decodeHex32, parseSimple, hyphen, parseHyphenated, urnPrefix from this file: read them. To trust the theorems of Computational/Theorems/Main.lean (CliElfLib), also read errTag. Everything else here is checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.This file gives the separation-logic predicates that say how the Rust values the triple-ratchet entry points (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalTripleRatchet.lean) read and write are laid out in memory, relating bytes to the session model (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet/LibsignalSession.lean). It extends LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalSessionRepr.lean (whose predicates it reuses: chainAt, messageKeyAt, optChainKeyAt, VecGhost, VecPin, ...). The trusted part is the pure address-parsing model: uuidTryParse (uuid 1.26.1's Uuid::try_parse), serviceIdParse (libsignal's ServiceId::parse_from_service_id_string) and the per-address bytes addrBytes, from which serializeAddresses builds the sender / recipient address bytes.Every layout fact below was read off the disassembly of the session image; the evidence is cited by instruction offset at each definition. Independently, a Rust replica of the types (110 const assertions compiled by rustc 1.98.1 for aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu) confirms every offset, size and niche, including the fields no instruction touches. The niche choices (-1 for a single-niche Option, -2 for Result<Vec<u8>, SPE>::Ok, 2^63 + i for the SPE) depend on the rustc version: rerun the replica on any toolchain bump. Kernel-checked guards: issStr_lengths, macFailedStr_length, pniPrefix_word.Glossary: SPE = SignalProtocolError (48 bytes); SS = proto::storage::SessionStructure (the payload of SessionState, 344 bytes); OTR / TR = OutgoingTripleRatchet (200 bytes) / TripleRatchet; IK = identity key; srk = sender ratchet key; pq / SPQR = the Sparse Post-Quantum Ratchet and its serialized state Vec<u8>; ISS = InvalidSessionStructure; UUID = universally unique identifier; ACI = Account Identity; PNI = Phone Number Identity; UTF-8 = 8-bit Unicode Transformation Format; LE = little-endian; RNG = random number generator; prost = the Rust protobuf (Protocol Buffers) library; niche = an invalid bit pattern of a field reused as an enum discriminant; sret = the caller-provided result buffer; Box<[u8]> = an owned byte slice (ptr, len) (no capacity word); rodata = the image's read-only data (↦ᵣ, duplicable read-only points-to).Niches* Option<Vec-like> (Option<Chain>, Option<chain::ChainKey>, Option<PendingPreKey>, Option<PendingKyberPreKey>, spqr::MessageKey): None = capacity word usize::MAX (noneCap). Evidence: OTR::apply_to_session_state 0xbd00: cmn x22, #1 (SS + 0x90); clear_unacknowledged_pre_key_message writes -1 to SS + 0xf8 and SS + 0x128.* SPE: word 0 = 2^63 + variant index for every non-dataful variant (speWord).* Result<Vec<u8>, SPE> (try_decrypt): Ok = word 0 -2 (okTagVec), the Vec at +8; Err = the SPE inline at +0. Result<T, SPE> for T = SignalMessage / OutgoingTripleRatchet: Ok = the payload at +0; Err = word 0 -1 (errTag), the SPE at +8.What is not modelled (framed byte-exactly by the ghost)alice_base_key, the registration ids, the pending pre-key's ids / timestamp, the whole pending_kyber_pre_key, and every Vec the session model does not determine (VecGhost).Main definitions* uuidTryParse (with hexNibble, decodeHex, decodeHex32, parseSimple, parseHyphenated, hyphen, urnPrefix): the model of uuid 1.26.1's Uuid::try_parse.* serviceIdParse (with tryParseHyphenated, hyphenatedLength, pniPrefix): the model of ServiceId::parse_from_service_id_string; ServiceIdParser / UuidParser are their types.* addrBytes, kindByte, serializeAddresses: the 36-byte sender / recipient encoding (serialize_addresses).* utf8: a string literal's UTF-8 bytes.* noneCap, errTag, okTagVec, speWord: the niche / tag words.* speAt, vecResultAt, errResultAt, signalMessageResultAt, otrResultAt: an SPE and the Result buffers of the entry points.* signalMessageAt, protocolAddressAt, sessionStateAt, otrAt: a SignalMessage, a ProtocolAddress, a SessionStructure and an OutgoingTripleRatchet in memory (with their ghost structures).* tapeAt, tapeAfter, coinsOf, decryptDraws: the HookRng random tape and how much of it a decrypt draws.* SessionGhost.AfterTryDecrypt, SessionGhost.AgreeExceptPq, SessionGhost.AfterOutgoingRatchetApply: how the ghost layout may change across each entry point.\n\n\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 101–103): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.noneCap\n\ndef errTagL105–107 ↗#The Err tag word of Result<T, SPE> for the 120 / 200-byte payloads (-1; try_from 0x258a4: str x9, [x19], OTR::from_session_state 0xb93c, OTR::encrypt 0x34058).\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 109–116): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.okTagVec, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.speWord\n\ndef utf8L118–119 ↗#A string literal's UTF-8 bytes.\n\n16 declarations not shown (lines 121–316): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.invalidSessionStructureStr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.issStr_lengths, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.macFailedStr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.macFailedStr_length, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.strRefAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.boxU8At, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.optBoxU8At, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.invalidMessageStrAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.speAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.vecResultAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.errResultAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost.ctPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost.serPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost.pqCap, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost.pqPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SignalMessageGhost.addrPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.signalMessageAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.signalMessageResultAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.AddrGhost.device, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.AddrGhost.name, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.AddrGhost, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.protocolAddressAt\n\nabbrev ServiceIdParserL318–321 ↗#A ServiceId parser on name bytes: the ServiceId of a name, or none. The Bool is the kind (false = Aci, tag 0; true = Pni, tag 1), the vector the UUID. The session specs use serviceIdParse uuidParse (below), the model of ServiceId::parse_from_service_id_string.\n\nabbrev UuidParserL323–325 ↗#A uuid::Uuid::try_parse on the input bytes: the 16 UUID bytes (Ok), or none (Err). The session specs are generic in it; the statement instantiates them at uuidTryParse.\n\nuuid::Uuid::try_parse (uuid 1.26.1)Upstream: ~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/uuid-1.26.1/src/parser.rs. Uuid::try_parse(&str) (:110–112) = try_parse_ascii(input.as_bytes()) (:136–144), which maps try_parse (:147–163) to Ok(Uuid::from_bytes(bytes)) (the 16 bytes, unchanged) or to Err(Error(ErrorKind::ParseOther)) on every failure. try_parse dispatches on the length:* 32 bytes: parse_simple (:188–225), 32 hex digits;* 36 bytes: parse_hyphenated (:228–278), the whole input;* 38 bytes {…}: parse_hyphenated of the 36 bytes inside the braces (:156);* 45 bytes urn:uuid:…: parse_hyphenated of the 36 bytes after the prefix (:157);* any other length or shape: Err (:161). decode_hex32 (:283–322) maps '0'..='9' to 0..9 and 'a'..='f' / 'A'..='F' (via | 0x20) to 10..15, and rejects every other byte; byte j of the UUID is (nibble 2j << 4) | nibble 2j+1.libsignal only reaches the 36-byte (hyphenated) path: try_parse_hyphenated (core/src/address.rs:264–271, tryParseHyphenated below) checks the length is hyphenatedLength = 36 before it calls Uuid::try_parse. The other shapes are modelled so that uuidTryParse is the whole function, as compiled into the image.\n\ndef hexNibbleL346–351 ↗#decode_hex32's per-byte map (src/parser.rs:289–305): digit = c - '0' if < 10, else alpha = (c | 0x20) - 'a' if < 6, as alpha + 10; any other byte is bad (none).\n\ndef decodeHexL353–361 ↗#decode_hex32's byte assembly (src/parser.rs:310–321) on a list of hex digits: each pair (hi, lo) gives (hi << 4) | lo; none if any digit is bad.\n\ndef decodeHex32L363–365 ↗#decode_hex32(hex: &[u8; 32]) (src/parser.rs:283–322): the 16 bytes, or none.\n\ndef parseSimpleL367–369 ↗#parse_simple(s, _) (src/parser.rs:188–225): 32 hex digits.\n\ndef hyphenL371–372 ↗#The byte '-'.\n\ndef parseHyphenatedL374–381 ↗#parse_hyphenated(s) (src/parser.rs:228–278): 36 bytes, '-' at positions 8, 13, 18 and 23 (:244–247), the other 32 bytes hex digits, gathered in order (:251–272).\n\ndef urnPrefixL383–384 ↗#The prefix urn:uuid: (src/parser.rs:157).\n\ndef uuidTryParseL386–395 ↗#uuid::Uuid::try_parse (uuid 1.26.1, src/parser.rs:110–112, 136–163; the shapes are listed above): some u = Ok(Uuid::from_bytes(u)), none = Err(ParseOther).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 397–400): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement uses_private.0.CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.uuidOf\n\ndef pniPrefixL402–403 ↗#The prefix \"PNI:\" of a PNI's string form (core/src/address.rs:273).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 405–409): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.pniPrefix_word\n\ndef hyphenatedLengthL411–412 ↗#uuid::fmt::Hyphenated::LENGTH (uuid 1.26.1, src/fmt.rs:366).\n\ndef tryParseHyphenatedL414–418 ↗#try_parse_hyphenated (core/src/address.rs:264–271): Uuid::try_parse(input).ok() if the input has exactly hyphenatedLength bytes, else None without calling it.\n\ndef serviceIdParseL420–430 ↗#ServiceId::parse_from_service_id_string (core/src/address.rs:263–280) over a Uuid::try_parse: if the input starts with \"PNI:\" (input.strip_prefix(\"PNI:\"), a byte-prefix test), the PNI of the rest; otherwise the ACI of the whole input; each None unless the parsed part has exactly 36 bytes and uuidParse accepts it. In the image this and Uuid::try_parse are in-image code (libsignal-core and uuid are whole crates), verified against serviceIdParse uuidTryParse.\n\ndef kindByteL432–436 ↗#ServiceIdKind's byte: the tag of the 17-byte Option<ServiceId> that the in-image parse_from_service_id_string writes (Aci = 0, +0x50: strb wzr, [x19]; Pni = 1, +0xb4..+0xb8: mov w8, #1; strb w8, [x19]; None = 2, +0x98..+0x9c), stored unchanged by serialize_addresses (core/src/address.rs's ServiceIdKind order).\n\ndef addrBytesL438–441 ↗#The 18 bytes kind ‖ uuid ‖ device of one address (ServiceId::service_id_fixed_width_binary, core/src/address.rs:212–216, then the device).\n\n16 declarations not shown (lines 443–657): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.serializeAddresses, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.tapeAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.tapeAfter, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.coinsOf, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.drawsCoins, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.decryptDraws, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost.priv, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost.srk, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost.mkPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost.ck, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SenderChainGhost.mkCap, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.optSenderChainAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.TailVecGhost.tail, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.TailVecGhost, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.TailVecGhost.vec, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.optTailVecAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.kyber, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.sender, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.pq, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.remoteIk, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.chains, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.root, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.chainsCap, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.localIk, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.alice, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.chainsPtr, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.regIds, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.pending, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.sessionStateAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.Keeps, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.SessionGhost.AfterTryDecrypt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.otrAt, CryptoVerification.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.otrResultAt\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalTripleRatchetRepr"}});